==English==
{{was wotd|2019|November|25}}

===Etymology===
From Late {{inh|en|enm|vinous}}, {{m|enm|vinose|t=consisting of, containing, or made of wine}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=vīnǒus|pos=adj|id=MED51192}}</ref> from {{der|en|la|vīnōsus|t=fond of wine; wine-flavoured}}, from {{m|la|vīnum|t=wine}}<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=adj|id=223586|date=1917|nodot=yes}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=adj}}</ref> + {{m|la|-ōsus|pos=''{{glossary|adjective}}-forming suffix meaning ‘full of, prone to’''}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈvaɪnəs/}}
* {{hyphenation|en|vin|ous}}
{{rfap|en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Pertaining to or having the [[characteristic#Noun|characteristics]] of [[wine]].
#* {{RQ:Sterne Sentimental Journey|volume=I|chapter=Preface in the Desobligeant|pages=29–30|pageref=30|passage=The man who firſt tranſplanted the grape of Burgundy to the Cape of Good Hope (obſerve he was a Dutchman) never dreamt of drinking the ſame wine at the Cape, that the ſame grape produced upon the French mountains—he was too phlegmatic for that—but undoubtedly he expected to drink ſome ſort of '''vinous''' liquor; [...]}}
## [[involve|Involving]] the [[use#Noun|use]] of wine.
##: {{synonyms|en|vinaceous|q=containing wine}}
##* {{RQ:Dickens Hard Times|chapter=Husband and Wife|pages=127–128|pageref=128|passage=The bride, in passing down stairs, dressed for her journey, found Tom waiting for her—flushed, either with his feelings, or the '''vinous''' part of the breakfast.}}
## Having the [[colour#Noun|colour]] of [[red wine]]; [[vinaceous]].
##* '''1854''', {{w|Thomas Mayne Reid}}, ''The Young Voyageurs, or, The Boy Hunters in the North'', Chapter 21,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/23129/23129-h/23129-h.htm]</sup>
##*: [...] François’ quick eye detected the presence of some very small birds moving among the blossoms. They were at once pronounced to be humming-birds, and of that species known as the “ruby-throats” (''Trochilus rolubris''), so called, because a flake of a beautiful '''vinous''' colour under the throat of the males exhibits, in the sun, all the glancing glories of the ruby.
# [[tend|Tending]] to [[drink#Verb|drink]] [[wine]] [[excessively]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1855|title=Peeps from a Belfry; Or: The Parish Sketch Book|author=Frederick William Shelton|page=146|isbn=|passage=Yet fat and '''vinous''' old Jack Falstaff, whose portraiture is the happiest hit in all the varied range of English comedy, must be sought for in other scenes.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1883|title=Fun - Volumes 37-38|author=|page=168|isbn=|passage=Curiosity induced him to ask the wild-eyed '''vinous''' old man if he knew the lady.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1898|title=Punch - Volume 114|author=|page=309|isbn=|passage=OLD SIMON the Soaker now keeps a rare store Of Malmsey and Malvoisie In tub-fuls of hundreds of litres or more, For a '''vinous''' old soul is he}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1899|title=Letter to HT Baker (in Raymond Asquith: Life and Letters, 1980)|author=Raymond Asquith|page=|isbn=|passage= It is one of the most trying things about this life, this necessity of laughing uproariously when '''vinous''' old men say things that are dirty but not funny; else one is written down as a prig.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|title=The Visitor|author=Christopher Chase Walker|page=|isbn=1785355368|passage=She was found wounded and amnesic by a '''vinous''' old farmer who, charitable and eccentric (or just radiantly bonkers), nursed her back to health in some ramshackle barn or outbuilding of his after the local Gendarmerie had investigated, photographed, swept up and hosed down the crash scene.}}
# [[affect#Verb|Affected]] by the [[drinking#Noun|drinking]] of wine.
#* '''1821''', {{w|Lord Byron}}, ''[[w:Don Juan (Byron)|Don Juan]]'', Canto III,<sup>[http://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks07/0700141.txt]</sup>
#*: The '''vinous''' Greek to whom he had address’d
#*: His question, much too merry to divine
#*: The questioner, fill’d up a glass of wine,
#* '''1848''', {{w|William Makepeace Thackeray}}, ''[[w:Vanity Fair (novel)|Vanity Fair]]'', Chapter 34,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/599/599-h/599-h.htm]</sup>
#*: “Come, come,” said James, putting his hand to his nose and winking at his cousin with a pair of '''vinous''' eyes, “no jokes, old boy; no trying it on on me. [...]”
#* '''1927''', {{w|Edgar Wallace}}, ''The Man Who Was Nobody'', Chapter 23,<sup>[http://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks11/1100581.txt]</sup>
#*: Once she had been kissed by a man in wine (the memory recalled Lady Tynewood and the parties she gave) and she had never forgotten the hated smell of that '''vinous''' breath.
# [[induce|Induced]] by the drinking of wine.
#* {{RQ:Hardy Tess|volume=I|chapter=IV|phase=1|pages=40–41|pageref=41|passage=In a large bedroom upstairs, the window of which was thickly curtained with a great woollen shawl lately discarded by the landlady, Mrs. Rolliver, were gathered on this evening nearly a dozen persons, all seeking '''vinous''' bliss; all old inhabitants of the nearer end of Marlott, and frequenters of this retreat.}}
#* '''1925''', {{w|F. Scott Fitzgerald}}, ''{{w|The Great Gatsby}}'', Chapter 3,<sup>[http://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks02/0200041h.html]</sup>
#*: [...] she threw up her hands, sank into a chair and went off into a deep '''vinous''' sleep.
#* '''1928''', {{w|Robert Byron}}, ''The Station: Travels to the Holy Mountain of Greece'', Chapter 9,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.net.au/ebooks14/1403311h.html]</sup>
#*: Gripped by a '''vinous''' [[Pentecost|pentecost]], I launched into speech [...]
#* '''2010''', {{w|Christopher Hitchens}}, ''Hitch-22'', Atlantic 2011, p. 254:
#*: It was a moment of bathos and anticlimax; a poor sequel to my smoke-ringed, '''vinous''' reverie on American grandeur the previous night.

